To determine how long it will take Lydia to fill her pool, we need to consider both the rate at which water is being added to the pool and the rate at which water is leaking out. The net rate of water being added to the pool is the difference between the rate of water being added by the hose and the rate of water leaking out.

The rate of water being added by the hose is 1.6 gallons per minute, and the rate of water leaking out is 0.1 gallons per minute. Therefore, the net rate of water being added to the pool is:
 1.6 - 0.1 = 1.5 \text{ gallons per minute} 

The pool holds 60 gallons of water. To find out how long it will take to fill the pool, we divide the total capacity of the pool by the net rate of water being added:
 \frac{60 \text{ gallons}}{1.5 \text{ gallons per minute}} = 40 \text{ minutes} 

Thus, it will take Lydia 40 minutes to fill the pool.

